:: R5-SideQuest-ClipReview [release5]
<<scene "img/ui/clip-goes-viral.png" "Character development speedrun">>

Before I sleep, I do the thing I shouldn't do. I watch my own clips.

The main one, the viral one, is forty three seconds long. It starts with me adjusting the wig, looking down at the chat, and then looking directly at the camera. The moment of eye contact is what did it. Apparently my expression shifted in a way I wasn't conscious of. The nervous energy dropped out and something else surfaced. Something open and raw and a little scared and a lot alive.

The comments under the clip.

"The way his whole energy changes when he looks at the camera. That's not a bit." "this is genuinely the most interesting thing to happen on twitch in months." "bro found himself on stream." "she's* lol"

That last comment, the casual correction, she, used naturally, without asterisks or qualifications, the way you'd correct someone who got a street name wrong. Matter of fact, like it was obvious.

I scroll deeper.

A thread compiling screenshots of my stream, the beginning, where I'm fidgeting and awkward, and the end, three hours later, where I'm leaning back in the chair with my legs crossed at the ankle and the skirt draped over my thighs and I'm laughing at something chat said and I look comfortable. Side by side, the transformation isn't the makeup or the wig. It's the posture. The face. The ease.

"This guy went from I lost a bet to I found myself in three hours. Character development speedrun."

Eight thousand likes.

I put the phone down. Pick it up. Put it down. My brain is doing the thing where it plays the same three seconds of footage on loop. My face in the mirror when Jess put the wig on. The unfinished sentence. The viewer count climbing like a launched rocket. Good girl. My voice cracking. The skirt on my thighs. The eight forty seven. The silence of the apartment. Jess's text from Marcus's apartment.

All of it. On loop. Like a song I can't skip.

